 ### Step 2: the German assessment of a commuter (German resident)
 
 - All the pay, including pay for days worked in Switzerland, is German employment income. Compute it as for any employee (de-employee-income-tax): gross pay less Werbungskosten or the employee lump sum, then special expenses.
-- The 2026 tariff applies to taxable income, with the solidarity surcharge at 5.5% above the EUR 20,350 threshold of income tax (see de-employee-income-tax).
+- The 2026 tariff applies to taxable income. For ordinary assessment without a separate § 32d(3)/(4) component, establish the actual Soli base and threshold: EUR 20,350 for a single assessment, EUR 40,700 for the statutory joint cases. Up to that threshold the ordinary surcharge is zero; above it take the smaller of 5.5% of the base and 11.9% of its excess over that threshold, dropping fractions of a cent. A stipulated ordinary single base of EUR 20,400 gives min(EUR 1,122, EUR 5.95) = EUR 5.95. Use the employee companion for child-related base and special § 32d selectors; Swiss § 36 withholding credit is a settlement credit, not a reduction of this Soli assessment base.
 - The Swiss tax withheld, up to 4.5% of gross, is credited against German income tax as if it were German withholding (art. 15a(3)(a); § 36(2) no. 2 EStG). It is not capped under § 34c. The employer's certificate of gross pay and Swiss tax is the evidence (protocol no. 6).
 - The Swiss employer withholds the lower of 4.5% and the Swiss tariff tax (Gre-1 note 6(b)). The commuter gives the employer the Gre-1 when starting work and when changing employer; the German tax office then renews it each year without a new application (Gre-1 notes 2 and 7).
 
 ### Step 3: when commuter status fails
 
-- Pay for work days in Switzerland: Swiss tax under art. 15; exempt in Germany with progression (art. 24(1) no. 1(c), no. 2; § 32b EStG), only with proof of Swiss tax paid (§ 50d(8)).
+- After testing art. 15(2) and other actual special rules above, qualifying Swiss workday pay under art. 15(1) is exempt in Germany with progression (art. 24(1) no. 1(c), no. 2; § 32b), on proof of Swiss waiver or payment of assessed Swiss tax (§ 50d(8)). If the three art. 15(2) gates instead hold, do not exempt that pay merely because work occurred in Switzerland.
 - Pay for days worked in Germany or third countries: taxable in Germany.
 - If the employer finds during the year that status will fail, it must collect the full Swiss tax (Gre-1 note 4).
 
 ### Step 4: Swiss occupational pensions of former commuters
 
-A former commuter who worked in the Swiss public service and draws a second-pillar pension keeps the Germany-first rule: the Swiss pension fund withholds at most 4.5% on a Gre-4 certificate (Gre-5 for survivors), and Germany credits it in proportion to the taxable share of the pension (BMF letters of 8 February 2018 and 7 December 2023; protocol no. 9).
+Protocol no. 9 covers Swiss second-pillar insurance payments where the last or current employment relevant to that insurance relationship before payment/start was in Swiss public service, including the specified vested-benefit accounts/policies. For a recipient who has or had art. 15a commuter status, art. 19(4) gives residence-state priority and the other state limits withholding under art. 15a. For recurring payments, give the second-pillar institution the prescribed residence certificate before the first receipt (Gre-4, or Gre-5 for survivors); capital payments instead require commuter-status certification in the prescribed official refund form. Merely having worked in Swiss public service sometime is insufficient. German credit of Swiss withholding requires the corresponding receipts actually to enter the German tax base and no refund to have been applied for or made. Preserve the actual pension classification/taxable-share limit: the cited 2018 method credits at most the corresponding 4.5% share for the Obligatorium or the relevant Ertragsanteil for the Überobligatorium; no universal pension percentage or double recovery is inferred.
 
