 ## Penalties and interest
 
-- **Late filing surcharge (§ 152 AO).** 0.25% per started month of the assessed tax less prepayments and credited withholding, at least EUR 25 per month, at most EUR 25,000. Compulsory if the 2026 return is not filed by 29 February 2028 and tax is due.
+- **Late filing surcharge (§ 152 AO).** 0.25% per started month of the assessed tax less prepayments and credited withholding, at least EUR 25 per month, at most EUR 25,000. The § 152(2) compulsory route after 29 February 2028 is subject to § 152(3): it does not apply where the office extends the deadline under § 109, including retroactively, where assessed tax is zero or negative, or where assessed tax does not exceed assessed prepayments plus creditable withholding. These exceptions limit paragraph 2 compulsion; apply paragraph 1's separate discretionary conditions, not an automatic waiver of every surcharge.
 - **Interest on assessed tax (§ 233a, § 238 AO).** 0.15% per full month on the balance, from 1 April 2028 for 2026.
 - **Late payment (§ 240 AO).** 1% per started month on overdue tax, rounded down to the nearest EUR 50.
-- **Lost commuter status found late.** The employer must collect the Swiss tax not withheld (Gre-1 note 4). In Germany the exemption under art. 24 is refused until the Swiss tax is shown paid (§ 50d(8) EStG), so the return is assessed with the pay taxed and changed later.
+- **Lost commuter status found late.** The employer must collect the Swiss tax not withheld (Gre-1 note 4). In Germany where art. 24 exemption actually applies after the other treaty tests, § 50d(8) requires proof either of Swiss waiver or that Swiss assessed tax was paid. If the required proof is first supplied after the German assessment included the pay, amend that assessment to the corresponding extent; do not demand payment of tax where a valid waiver is proved.
 
 Figures and sections as in de-non-resident-tax, which read them on 2026-10-23.
 
 ## Cross-border points
 
 - **Two treaty layers from 2026.** The protocol of 21 August 2023 rewrote the commuter points into the treaty protocol (nos. 5 to 7) and ended the 1991 negotiation record (protocol no. 7). The 20% regular-return test is the main new condition.
-- **Swiss withholding for German residents, dividends, interest.** Switzerland withholds 35% Verrechnungssteuer; under art. 10 a German resident can recover down to 15% (0% for a German company holding at least 10% for 365 days); interest is taxable only in Germany (art. 11). Refund route in ch-cross-border.
+- **Swiss withholding for German residents, dividends, interest.** Switzerland withholds 35% Verrechnungssteuer. For the treaty-resident beneficial owner, the ordinary residual dividend ceiling is 15% under art. 10(2)(c); separately classify the Rhine border-power-company 5% population and the specified deductible silent-participation/profit-sharing instruments under the 30% branch in (a)/(b). Art. 10(3) gives source-state exemption only to a beneficially entitled treaty-resident company other than a partnership, directly holding at least 10% for a 365-day period including the dividend payment day; the stated merger/division/conversion changes are disregarded for that period. Indirect 10%, partnership ownership or an unproved payment-inclusive period does not establish zero. Its German REIT/fund exclusion concerns German payers; do not extend it to Swiss payers. Actual entitlement and evidence precede any Swiss refund, whose route is in ch-cross-border; German outbound cases use the treaty-withholding companion. Interest follows art. 11's separate Germany-only route.
 - **German withholding on payments to Swiss residents.** See de-treaty-withholding-rates.
 - **Social security.** EU-Switzerland coordination decides where contributions are due, separately from tax. Not covered here.
 - **Inheritance tax.** A separate Germany-Switzerland inheritance tax treaty of 1978 exists. Not covered here.
